# How to cite rebasis.
#
# GitHub reads this file to render "Cite this repository", and citation
# managers read it directly. It is validated in CI by `tools/check_citations.py`
# alongside the arXiv references in `docs/`, on the same principle: a citation
# nobody can resolve is not a citation.
#
# There is deliberately no `version:` or `date-released:` field. `pyproject.toml`
# derives the version from the git tag and says so — "the version is derived
# from the git tag; it is never stored by hand" — and a second copy here would
# be a second source that drifts, silently, in the direction of whichever one
# nobody looked at. Cite the tag you installed; `pip show rebasis` prints it.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use rebasis in academic work, please cite it using the metadata
  below.
title: rebasis
abstract: >-
  A tool that measures whether an embedding-model upgrade is worth taking,
  fits an adapter that maps the new model's query vectors into the space an
  existing index already uses when it is, and migrates the index safely when
  the user is ready. The decision is grounded in the user's own corpus and
  query log rather than in a benchmark average, and the tool recommends
  against bridging more often than for it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Zengin
    given-names: Batuhan Zorbey
    email: batuhanzorbeyzengin@gmail.com
repository-code: https://github.com/batuhanzorbeyzengin/rebasis
url: https://batuhanzorbeyzengin.github.io/rebasis/
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - embeddings
  - vector-database
  - information-retrieval
  - index-migration
  - procrustes
  - representation-alignment
